---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/brevo-vs-mailchimp';
const title = 'Brevo vs Mailchimp for Shopify Email: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = 'Brevo vs Mailchimp for Shopify — CRM and transactional breadth vs brand familiarity, Shopify automation depth, pricing, and migration timing.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Is Brevo or Mailchimp better for a Shopify store leaving legacy email?',
    answer: 'Evaluate the current plans against required Shopify events, lifecycle branches, CRM pipelines, transactional APIs and campaign editing. Verify account support for each requirement instead of inferring performance or compliance from company location or brand familiarity. This comparison establishes no universal winner or measured retention advantage.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Brevo vs Mailchimp pricing compare at 8,000 contacts?',
    answer: 'Request matched current quotes at 8,000 contacts using the same send volume, destinations, message segments and required modules. Include platform and carrier fees, minimums, discounts, integration and labor. Contact count alone does not establish the cheaper stack; compare all-in costs and contribution margin under equal assumptions.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which has better Shopify cart abandonment?',
    answer: 'Neither is best-in-class versus Omnisend or Klaviyo. Brevo builds competent cart workflows with more manual setup. Mailchimp\'s Shopify automation improved but still treats ecommerce as an add-on — product blocks and trigger timing lag dedicated platforms. If cart recovery is primary KPI, consider graduating to Omnisend, Sequenzy, or Klaviyo after Brevo or Mailchimp.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I migrate from Mailchimp to Brevo without losing segments?',
    answer: 'Inspect current export and import support for tags, segment rules, permission evidence and suppression records. Rebuild and validate workflows on test records before cutover; no fixed two-to-four-week schedule or automatic journey portability is established. Preserve purpose/channel permission and current purchase, subscription and suppression state. Assign one sender and queue owner per flow, cancel outstanding messages in the old system and test replay deduplication before enabling the replacement.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I also consider Sequenzy instead of Brevo or Mailchimp?',
    answer: 'Sequenzy fits Shopify lifecycle strategy with pay-per-email pricing — often the right graduation path when Brevo and Mailchimp both feel like broadcast tools with automation bolted on. Brevo wins CRM breadth. Mailchimp wins creative familiarity. Sequenzy wins when you need welcome, cart, and winback playbooks live this month.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which handles transactional email better?',
    answer: 'Verify current transactional API or SMTP access, required modules, billing and integration behavior in each proposed account. Separate order-service messages from marketing by actual purpose and permission requirements. Test delivery events, idempotency, ownership and cancellation where applicable; a shared vendor or invoice does not establish equivalent capabilities or legal eligibility.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which is better for EU merchants?',
    answer: 'Compare actual processing terms, data locations, subprocessors, transfer arrangements, retention and consent records against the merchant requirements with appropriate review. EU company location alone does not establish GDPR compliance or a successful legal audit. Verify current account controls and the purpose-specific eligibility of imported contacts; no legal clearance is established here.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What workflow test should decide the winner?',
    answer: 'Build welcome, cart, post-purchase, winback, and one promotional broadcast. Score time-to-live, Shopify trigger accuracy, CRM needs, and total cost at projected list size. If CRM or transactional matters, lean Brevo. If creative campaigns and team familiarity matter, lean Mailchimp. If Shopify retention is the bottleneck, lean neither — test Sequenzy or Omnisend.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-cyan-100/90">/</span>
        <span class="text-cyan-100/90">Brevo vs Mailchimp</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Brevo vs Mailchimp for Shopify SMS</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">
          Consider proposed evaluations for a hypothetical $32k/month pottery studio familiar with Mailchimp since 2019, a German outdoor gear shop reviewing processing and permission records, and a US meal-kit brand assessing corporate-gifting CRM. These are not observed merchants or tests. Verify current plan, connector and product support separately; neither company location nor platform category establishes compliance or workflow superiority.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-cyan-100/90">This comparison weights SMS-native compliance, quiet hours, cost-per-message economics, and whether each platform treats texts as scarce interrupts — not email with fewer characters. Sequenzy is evaluated as the lifecycle email layer beside dedicated SMS senders.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'List pricing', tool: 'Brevo', reason: 'Often cheaper at 8k–20k contacts with send caps to watch.' },
            { label: 'Team familiarity', tool: 'Mailchimp', reason: 'Lower training cost when staff already knows the UI.' },
            { label: 'CRM + transactional', tool: 'Brevo', reason: 'Pipelines and API email without Mandrill add-on.' },
            { label: 'Creative campaigns', tool: 'Mailchimp', reason: 'Template gallery and Content Studio depth.' },
            { label: 'Also consider', tool: 'Sequenzy', reason: 'Shopify lifecycle playbooks when both feel like legacy broadcast tools.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Brevo</th><th>Mailchimp</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Best for</td><td>CRM + transactional + EU</td><td>Familiar broadcast marketing</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Starting price</td><td>Free tier; paid from ~$25/mo</td><td>Free tier; paid from ~$13/mo</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Billing model</td><td>Contacts + send limits</td><td>Contacts — aggressive scaling</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shopify depth</td><td>Good — manual automation</td><td>Moderate — improved but add-on feel</td></tr>
            <tr><td>CRM</td><td>Built-in pipelines</td><td>Basic — not core</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Transactional</td><td>Strong native API</td><td>Mandrill add-on</td></tr>
            <tr><td>GDPR tooling</td><td>Strong — EU native</td><td>Adequate</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Learning curve</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Low for veterans</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Why this comparison matters for Shopify merchants</h2>
        <p>
          Map the actual Shopify events, lifecycle branches, CRM and transactional workflows needed by the store. Verify current support in each proposed account rather than assuming that predictive analytics, browse events or SMS must live elsewhere. Team familiarity can be a selection criterion, but it does not establish measured retention performance or a universal need to migrate later.
        </p>
        <p>
          The pottery studio stayed on Mailchimp because the owner had used it since 2019 — migration labor exceeded savings until list pricing hit $187/mo at 11k contacts. Brevo quote at equivalent contacts: $78/mo. They switched in March, rebuilt two automations, and accepted that cart recovery would still underperform until they graduated to Sequenzy or Omnisend six months later.
        </p>

        <h2>Merchant scenario: German outdoor gear and GDPR</h2>
        <p>
          For a hypothetical $41k/month German outdoor gear store, propose a review of influencer-list provenance, purpose/channel permission, suppression and cross-border processing arrangements. Compare actual audit records and terms with the merchant checklist. No vendor has passed legal review here, and EU residency or consent logging alone does not establish compliance or justify a fixed migration duration.
        </p>
        <p>
          Propose equivalent cart workflows on current account test records and record setup effort only after testing. Confirm event freshness, product rendering and permission checks at execution. No four-day build time, relative preset advantage or compliance-led merchant decision was observed in this review.
        </p>

        <h2>Merchant scenario: meal-kit corporate gifting CRM</h2>
        <p>
          For a hypothetical meal-kit store with an illustrative 200 corporate-gifting inquiries per quarter, test lead stages from inquiry through sales call to bulk order. Verify current CRM modules and connector support before assuming another subscription is required. A shared contact record must preserve purpose-specific permission and suppression; an email click alone does not authorize every sales or marketing message.
        </p>

        <h2>Workflow test: welcome series</h2>
        <p>
          Propose a welcome-series test using equivalent signup events, templates, branch requirements and wholesale-interest tags in each current account. Record setup effort, trigger latency and rendering results only after testing. Verify channel permission and current suppression before execution; neither familiar UI nor additional builder steps establishes the faster or better workflow in advance.
        </p>

        <h2>Workflow test: abandoned cart</h2>
        <p>
          Propose matched cart tests using an illustrative $75 average order value, equivalent eligibility and clearly defined recovery windows. Check event freshness and product blocks alongside current purchase, subscription and suppression state. Assign one queue and sender owner, cancel work after purchase or opt-out and test replay deduplication. No 8–12% versus 14–18% recovery result or relative latency was observed; distinguish attribution from randomized causal lift with uncertainty.
        </p>

        <h2>Pricing reality at growing list sizes</h2>
        <p>
          Mailchimp punishes list growth — 20k contacts on Standard often exceeds $280/mo. Brevo at 20k frequently lands $120–180/mo with send volume caps to monitor. The pottery studio's switch saved $1,300 annually — funded partial Omnisend trial later. Calculate 18-month projected list size before choosing; Mailchimp's footer pricing misleads.
        </p>

        <h2>Team fit: who should choose which</h2>
        <p><strong>Choose Brevo if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>EU GDPR and consent audit trails are procurement requirements</li>
          <li>You need CRM pipelines or transactional API email</li>
          <li>List pricing at 10k+ contacts makes Mailchimp uneconomic</li>
          <li>Marketing and transactional should share one vendor</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Choose Mailchimp if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Team familiarity reduces migration and training cost</li>
          <li>Creative campaigns and template gallery are primary workflow</li>
          <li>List size stays under ~8k contacts near term</li>
          <li>CRM and transactional are handled elsewhere</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Also consider Sequenzy</h2>
        <p>
          Brevo and Mailchimp are stepping stones, not destinations, for Shopify retention at scale. Sequenzy offers guided lifecycle playbooks with pay-per-email pricing — the graduation path when you have outgrown broadcast tools but Klaviyo profile pricing feels premature. Keep Brevo CRM if sales teams depend on it; move DTC lifecycle to Sequenzy.
        </p>

        <h2>Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          Brevo wins CRM, transactional, EU compliance, and list economics at scale. Mailchimp wins familiarity and creative campaign velocity for smaller lists. Neither wins Shopify retention maximums — plan a graduation path to Omnisend, Sequenzy, or Klaviyo when cart and winback become line items on your P&L. Pick the generalist that matches today's constraints without ignoring tomorrow's retention maturity.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/brevo-vs-omnisend">Brevo vs Omnisend</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/drip-vs-mailchimp">Drip vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-mailchimp">Sequenzy vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/food-and-beverage">Email apps for food & beverage</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-12">
      <h2>TCPA, consent, and cost-per-message discipline</h2>
      <p>
          Document purpose-, destination-, sender- and channel-specific permission requirements and the wording and source of opt-in. Verify current subscription and suppression state and applicable timing rules before queued execution. Do not assume one universal consent standard or that vendor tooling establishes legal compliance. Obtain appropriate review of checkout, popup and keyword disclosures before launch.
        </p>
      <p>Model cost per delivered message before scaling broadcasts. A recovered cart that required 25% off plus three texts is often negative contribution margin even when attribution dashboards look green. Pair urgent SMS with Sequenzy email when the message needs education, social proof, or multi-product context — never duplicate the same offer on both channels within twenty-four hours unless the subscriber opted into high-frequency updates.</p>
      <p>Weekly ops: audit STOP/HELP handling, reply inbox backlog, opt-out spikes by flow, and suppression overlap with email cart recovery. Quarterly: re-read popup and checkout consent language against live automations. TCPA litigation and carrier filtering both punish stores that treat SMS like promotional email with shorter copy.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>SMS operations depth 1: TCPA express written consent…</h2>
      <p>For <strong>compare brevo vs mailchimp</strong>, TCPA express written consent and opt-in source documentation is not optional infrastructure. Shopify SMS apps that recover urgent revenue without wrecking consent treat every text as a scarce interrupt — cart recovery after meaningful checkout intent, launch access for opted-in VIPs, replenishment when SKU usage data supports timing.</p>
      <p>Postscript remains the SMS-native benchmark on this site: TCPA tooling, two-way replies, Shopify event triggers, and broadcast controls. Sequenzy is deliberately ranked as lifecycle email layer beside SMS — welcome depth, post-purchase education, winback arcs that should not be crammed into 160 characters. Klaviyo and Omnisend fit when one team wants bundled SMS with segmentation; Attentive when enterprise acquisition economics justify custom contracts.</p>
      <p>Run the math before scaling: platform fee plus carrier fee plus average discount per recovered order. A text that needs 25% off to convert may be negative contribution margin even when attribution dashboards look green. Pair channels deliberately — email for proof and education, SMS for timing — and never duplicate the same offer within twenty-four hours unless the subscriber opted into high-frequency updates.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Document opt-in source, message type promised, and maximum weekly frequency per segment.</li>
        <li>Configure quiet hours before enabling automations; audit checkout and popup copy quarterly.</li>
        <li>Assign one owner for reply inbox, suppression maps, and pre-launch TCPA review.</li>
        <li>Measure opt-out rate by flow, cost per incremental order, and full-price repeat purchase quality.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>SMS operations depth 2: quiet hours configured by…</h2>
      <p>For <strong>compare brevo vs mailchimp</strong>, quiet hours configured by customer timezone not HQ timezone is not optional infrastructure. Shopify SMS apps that recover urgent revenue without wrecking consent treat every text as a scarce interrupt — cart recovery after meaningful checkout intent, launch access for opted-in VIPs, replenishment when SKU usage data supports timing.</p>
